      </div>

    </section>



    <!-- =====================================================
         04 — CONTENT ARCHITECTURE
    ====================================================== -->

    <section class="newsletter-section shell">

      <div class="section-heading">

        <p class="eyebrow">
          Schneider Electric / Content Architecture
        </p>

        <div class="section-rule">
          <span>⌁</span>
        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="newsletter-section__intro">

        <h2 class="display">
          Stories Change.<br />
          <span>Systems Hold.</span>
        </h2>

        <p>
          Although the exact content changed every month, the newsletter
          repeatedly needed to balance several types of activity without
          allowing any single issue to become visually or editorially chaotic.
        </p>

      </div>


      <div class="newsletter-category-grid">

        {
          contentCategories.map((category, index) => (

            <article>

              <span>
                {String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}
              </span>

              <h3>{category}</h3>

            </article>

          ))
        }

      </div>

    </section>

    <!-- =====================================================
         06 — ISSUE DECONSTRUCTION
    ====================================================== -->

    <section class="newsletter-section shell">

      <div class="section-heading">

        <p class="eyebrow">
          Schneider Electric / Issue Deconstruction
        </p>

        <div class="section-rule">
          <span>⌁</span>
        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="newsletter-section__intro">

        <h2 class="display">
          Inside an<br />
          <span>Issue.</span>
        </h2>

        <p>
          A finished newsletter is the visible output, but the underlying work
          is deciding what leads, what supports it and how much context each
