    <!-- =====================================================
     10 — CYNLR DEEP DIVE
    ====================================================== -->

    <section class="newsletter-section shell">

      <div class="section-heading">

        <p class="eyebrow">
          CynLr / Editorial Deep Dive
        </p>

        <div class="section-rule">
          <span>⌁</span>
        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="newsletter-section__intro">

        <h2 class="display">
          Content pool to<br />
          <span>stakeholder angle.</span>
        </h2>

        <div class="newsletter-section__copy">

          <p>
            The deeper editorial task was not writing four versions of the same
            newsletter. It was deciding which stories mattered to each
            stakeholder, what those stories proved, how they should be ordered,
            and what action the reader should be encouraged to take next.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same update could therefore become a lead story for one audience,
            supporting proof for another, or disappear entirely when it did not
            answer that stakeholder's question.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="newsletter-deep-dive">

        <div class="newsletter-deep-dive__steps">

          <article>

            <span>01</span>

            <div>

              <p class="newsletter-kicker">
                Source / Content Pool
              </p>

              <h3 class="display">
                What evidence is available?
              </h3>

              <p>
                Start with the full pool of company activity: product releases,
                customer visits, partnerships, research collaborations, funding,
                events and expansion.
              </p>

            </div>

          </article>


          <article>

            <span>02</span>

            <div>

              <p class="newsletter-kicker">
                Stakeholder Question
              </p>

              <h3 class="display">
                What does this audience need proved?
              </h3>

              <p>
                Define the perception gap first. The editorial angle follows
                from the question the reader is trying to answer.
              </p>

            </div>

          </article>


          <article>

            <span>03</span>

            <div>

              <p class="newsletter-kicker">
                Proof Hierarchy
              </p>

              <h3 class="display">
                Which stories deserve priority?
              </h3>

              <p>
                Rank stories according to their value for that audience. Lead
                with the strongest proof signal, use secondary stories to
                reinforce credibility, and remove information that does not
                advance the argument.
              </p>

            </div>

          </article>


          <article>

            <span>04</span>

            <div>

              <p class="newsletter-kicker">
                Editorial Treatment
              </p>

              <h3 class="display">
                How should that proof be presented?
              </h3>

              <p>
                Adapt hierarchy, density, imagery, labels and calls to action
                to the reader: people-led for talent, metrics-led for investors,
                ecosystem-led for vendors and enterprise proof-led for customers.
              </p>

            </div>

          </article>

        </div>


        <div class="newsletter-deep-dive__visual">

          <img
            src="/images/newsletters/cynlr.webp"
            alt="CynLr stakeholder editorial strategy"
            loading="lazy"
          />

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>

